2. Core Design Principles
Principle 1 — Everything Is Cognition

All meaningful information participating in cognition SHALL exist as Cognitive Elements.

Examples:

Facts
Entities
Memories
Goals
Plans
Policies
Strategies
Capabilities
Intentions
Decisions

Nothing important exists outside the cognitive model.

Principle 2 — Provenance Is Mandatory

Every Cognitive Element SHALL answer:

Where did it originate?
When was it created?
What produced it?
What evidence supports it?
What transformations affected it?

No unexplained knowledge exists.

Principle 3 — Nothing Is Silently Deleted

MOS uses lifecycle transitions:

Active

↓

Archived

↓

Superseded

↓

Expired

History remains available.

Principle 4 — Events Are Immutable

Events represent historical reality.

Events:

cannot be edited,
cannot be rewritten,
cannot disappear.

Interpretations may change.

Events do not.

Principle 5 — Truth Is Versioned

MOS does not assume knowledge is static.

Facts evolve.

Policies evolve.

Strategies evolve.

The system preserves previous states.

3. Cognitive State Space

The Cognitive State Space is the complete collection of all Cognitive Elements.

Conceptually:

Cognitive State Space

├── Events
├── Entities
├── Facts
├── Relationships
├── Experiences
├── Goals
├── Plans
├── Skills
├── Procedures
├── Policies
├── Capabilities
├── Strategies
├── Intents
├── Decisions
└── Evaluations

Everything is connected through semantic relationships.

4. Cognitive Elements

A Cognitive Element is the fundamental unit of MOS.

Every element SHALL have:

Identity

Globally unique identifier.

Type

Semantic classification.

Provenance

Origin information.

Version

Historical evolution.

Lifecycle

Current state.

Relationships

Connections to other elements.

Metadata

Additional contextual information.

