6. Domains

Domains are semantic organizations of cognition.

Domains do not own cognition.

They project views over the Cognitive State Space.

Core domains:

Observation

Raw incoming information.

Knowledge

Validated understanding.

Attention

Currently active cognition.

Planning

Future-oriented cognition.

Execution

External interaction.

Reflection

Self-improvement.

Learning

Adaptation.

Prediction

Possible future states.

Governance

Control of cognition.

Archive

Low-activation historical cognition.

7. Meta-Cognitive Layer

MOS introduces Meta-Cognition.

Meta-Cognitive Elements describe, control, or improve cognition itself.

Examples:

Policies

Rules governing behavior.

Strategies

Methods of achieving capabilities.

Orchestrators

Systems that select strategies.

Ontologies

Models of meaning.

Evaluation Models

Models of quality.

Trust Models

Models of reliability.

Important rule:

The system that governs cognition is itself part of cognition.

8. Capability Layer

